D. What this permission does NOT cover -- open problem, now an opt-in
   operator's problem, not MOTRES's
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Intel MKL chain pulled in by `pypardiso` is not the two Licensed
Libraries alone. As installed by anyone who follows
`requirements-pardiso.txt` (2026-09-30 versions) it also includes
`intel-openmp` (2026.1.2) and `intel-cmplr-lib-ur` (2026.1.2), both
licensed under the "Intel End User License Agreement for Developer
Tools" (Version August 2024) -- a different, more restrictive agreement
than the Intel Simplified Software License covering (i) and (ii) above.
That agreement's section 3.1 states You may NOT "(x) modify, create a
Derivative Work, link, or distribute the Materials so that any part of
it becomes Reciprocal Open Source Software" or "(xi) use the Materials
directly or indirectly for SaaS services or service bureau purposes
(i.e., a service that allows use of or access to the Materials by a
third party as part of that service...)". Both clauses read, on their
face, as squarely describing combining these two components with the
AGPL-licensed Program and running the combination as a network service.
No permission MOTRES grants under AGPL section 7 can cure a restriction
in Intel's own licence -- that is Intel's grant, not MOTRES's, and
MOTRES cannot expand it. This file therefore deliberately does NOT name
`intel-openmp` or `intel-cmplr-lib-ur` in the grant of Section B.

Because the architecture decision above removes `pypardiso`/MKL from
MOTRES's own default image and default hosted service entirely, this is
no longer MOTRES's own compliance risk by default -- it becomes a
question for any operator who chooses to self-install and self-enable
the optional PARDISO backend on their own infrastructure, particularly
if they then also run it as their own network-facing service. Options
for such an operator, none endorsed or adopted here (see
docs/LICENSE-EXCEPTION-NOTES.md Section 6 for the underlying evidence
and docs/MESHER_TRANSITION.md's "Gmsh and MKL in the same process" note
for the related, now-optional-path-only, meshing-side analysis):
  1. Get Intel's explicit written permission for this specific use.
  2. Investigate configuring MKL to avoid loading `intel-openmp` and
     `intel-cmplr-lib-ur` at runtime (MKL supports alternate threading
     layers, e.g. `MKL_THREADING_LAYER=TBB` or `=SEQUENTIAL`, which may
     avoid the Intel-OpenMP runtime; not yet confirmed to work with
     `pypardiso` specifically, and it is not yet confirmed that
     `intel-cmplr-lib-ur` is avoidable the same way).
  3. Do not run the optional PARDISO backend as a network-facing
     service at all; keep it for a non-served, non-SaaS, local use only,
     where clause (xi) does not appear to bite.
This is documented for whoever opts in, and flagged for counsel on
whether documenting it this way is sufficient, not decided here.

